  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA cold front is pushing through the Northern Plains this afternoon, \nkeeping temperatures in the north in the 60s. Meanwhile ahead of the \nfront in far southeastern ND into west central MN, temperatures have \nreached the mid 80s, and dew points have climbed into the mid 60s, \ncreating a narrow, couple of county wide axis of instability. As the \ncold front propagates through during the late afternoon and evening \nhours, thunderstorms are anticipated to develop, some of which will \nbecome strong to severe. \n\nOver the weekend, a large upper low will dig into the Pacific \nNorthwest. Tracking eastward by late Sunday into Monday, widespread \nrain is forecasted for the Northern Plains. Behind this system, \nzonal flow will keep temperatures in the 60s most days. Rising \nheights late next week may bring a few 70s back into the forecast. \n\n...Severe Risk Today...\n\nA cold front pushing eastward will provide the catalyst for \nthunderstorm development late this afternoon into the evening hours. \nLatest satellite imagery shows a developing stratocumulus field\nahead of the front in Grant, Otter Tail and Wadena Counties. \nCapping is forecast to erode between 4 and 5 o'clock. As it \nerodes, the expectation is for storms to develop along the \nfront, which should then be situated in the western reaches of \nthe current SPC severe weather level 2 out of 5 risk area. \nStorms will have a narrow but rich axis of instability to work \nwith, with MLCAPE approaching 3000 J/Kg, and effective shear of \naround 40 knots. Meanwhile, across eastern South Dakota, a \nsurface low is propagating eastward/northeastward. In west \ncentral MN, closer to where this surface low will be located by \nthis evening, more low level curvature within hodographs will \nfoster the potential for a tornado or two. Further north \n(especially north of US Highway 2), hodographs are straighter, \nkeeping the severe risk tied more to a hail and wind threat \ninstead of tornado. One big question is how quickly storm mode \nbecomes messy. The front should provide strong enough forcing \nfor several updrafts to go up at once. If this occurs, the \nwindow for truly discrete storms will be shorter, leaving the \npotential for significant hail and a tornado or two shorter in \nduration. Activity will cluster and grow upscale as it pushes \neastward, leaving our FA around 10 PM this evening. \n\n...Late Weekend into Early Next Week Rainfall...\n\nA large upper low over the Pacific Northwest will propagate eastward \ntowards the Northern Plains late Sunday into Monday. Very little \ninstability appears in place, therefore severe weather is not \nexpected. However, as the low tracks from southwest to northeast \nacross the Northern Plains and Canadian Prairies, rainfall will \noverspread the region from west to east Sunday night, lasting into \nMonday. While there remains uncertainty in where the deformation\nband and heaviest rainfall will set up, with NBM probabilities \nfor a half inch of rain is about 60 percent across the entire \nFA.",
